    "markdown": "# Computer Graphics: Parallax Mapping\n\nWhile normal mapping creates realistic lighting over bumps, it is flat: if you view a normal-mapped surface at a sharp angle, the bumps have no thickness and do not occlude each other. To solve this limitation, we use **Parallax Mapping**.\n\nParallax mapping shifts the texture coordinates at each pixel based on the camera's view angle and a **Height Map** texture, simulating physical depth, self-occlusion, and parallax offsets.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Core Principle & Coordinate Shifting\n\nWhen looking at a bumpy surface, the camera's view ray hits a point on the physical height field that does not align with the flat triangle plane. \n\nTo simulate this depth:\n1.  The shader samples the height map at the initial UV coordinate.\n2.  It uses the height value ($h$) and the view vector $\\mathbf{V}$ in tangent space to calculate a coordinate offset $\\Delta \\mathbf{p}$.\n3.  It shifts the UV coordinates, sampling the diffuse and normal maps at the offset location.\n\n$$\\Delta \\mathbf{p} = \\left( \\frac{V_x}{V_z}, \\frac{V_y}{V_z} \\right) \\cdot h \\cdot s$$\n\nWhere $s$ is a user-defined depth scale factor.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Parallax Occlusion Mapping (POM)\n\nBasic parallax mapping assumes the height map is flat within the offset step. For steep bumps, this assumption fails, causing textures to stretch or look distorted.\n\nTo render deep, complex details (like deep brick crevices or cobblestones), we use **Parallax Occlusion Mapping (POM)**. Instead of a single offset step, POM performs **Raymarching inside the fragment shader**:\n\n1.  It divides the height field into a series of horizontal layers.\n2.  It steps along the view ray vector in tangent space, sampling the height map at each layer.\n3.  It stops when the ray's depth is greater than or equal to the sampled height value.\n4.  It performs a linear interpolation between the last two sample points to find the exact intersection point.\n\n<div style=\"margin: 20px 0; text-align: center;\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" width=\"100%\" height=\"220\" style=\"background: #141416; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #27272a;\">\n  <!-- Flat polygon plane -->\n  <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" />\n  <text x=\"60\" y=\"40\" fill=\"#71717a\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">Flat Triangle Surface (y=0)</text>\n  \n  <!-- Height Map curve boundary -->\n  <path d=\"M 50 160 Q 150 60 220 140 Q 300 190 350 90 Q 400 40 450 120\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <text x=\"180\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Height Field Profile</text>\n  \n  <!-- View Ray in Tangent Space -->\n  <line x1=\"120\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n  <circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"50\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n  <text x=\"100\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"10\">View Ray</text>\n  \n  <!-- Raymarch step points -->\n  <circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"69\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n  <circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"88\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n  \n  <!-- Intersection Point (Ray hits height curve) -->\n  <circle cx=\"242\" cy=\"108\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#10b981\" />\n  <text x=\"252\" y=\"112\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\">Intersection point (UV Offset)</text>\n</svg>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n## 3. Performance Cost\n\nPOM provides high-quality displacement mapping but has a high performance cost:\n*   **Shader Complexity:** Raymarching requires loops and multiple texture fetches per pixel, which can bottleneck the GPU.\n*   **Optimization:** The shader dynamically scales the number of ray steps based on the view angle (shooting more steps at steep angles, and fewer when looking straight down).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation Example (GLSL Fragment Shader)\n\nHere is a GLSL shader implementation of steep parallax mapping:\n\n```glsl\n#version 330 core\nout vec4 FragColor;\n\nin vec2 TexCoords;\nin vec3 TangentViewDir;\n\nuniform sampler2D diffuseMap;\nuniform sampler2D depthMap; // Height map (stored as depth values)\nuniform float heightScale;\n\nvec2 ParallaxMapping(vec2 texCoords, vec3 viewDir)\n{\n    // 1. Configure raymarch steps based on view angle\n    const float minLayers = 8.0;\n    const float maxLayers = 32.0;\n    float numLayers = mix(maxLayers, minLayers, abs(dot(vec3(0.0, 0.0, 1.0), viewDir)));\n    \n    // 2. Step size calculations\n    float layerDepth = 1.0 / numLayers;\n    float currentLayerDepth = 0.0;\n    \n    // Shift vector along view direction: P = (V.x, V.y) / V.z * scale\n    vec2 P = viewDir.xy / viewDir.z * heightScale;\n    vec2 deltaTexCoords = P / numLayers;\n    \n    vec2 currentTexCoords = texCoords;\n    float currentDepthMapValue = texture(depthMap, currentTexCoords).r;\n    \n    // 3. Raymarch loop\n    while(currentLayerDepth < currentDepthMapValue)\n    {\n        currentTexCoords -= deltaTexCoords;\n        currentDepthMapValue = texture(depthMap, currentTexCoords).r;\n        currentLayerDepth += layerDepth;\n    }\n    \n    return currentTexCoords; // Return the offset texture coordinate\n}\n\nvoid main()\n{\n    // Resolve parallax UV coordinates\n    vec3 V = normalize(TangentViewDir);\n    vec2 offsetCoords = ParallaxMapping(TexCoords, V);\n    \n    // Discard pixel if UV coordinates drift outside boundary\n    if(offsetCoords.x > 1.0 || offsetCoords.y > 1.0 || offsetCoords.x < 0.0 || offsetCoords.y < 0.0)\n        discard;\n        \n    vec3 color = texture(diffuseMap, offsetCoords).rgb;\n    FragColor = vec4(color, 1.0);\n}\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## 5. Conceptual Check questions\n\n**1. What is Parallax Mapping and what problem does it solve?**\n**Answer:**\nParallax mapping is a shader technique that shifts texture coordinates based on the view angle and a heightmap to simulate 3D depth.\n1) It solves the flatness limitation of normal mapping, where grooves and bumps look flat when viewed from the side.\n2) It works by shifting the UV coordinates of a pixel so the sampler reads from a different part of the texture.\n3) This simulates parallax: parts of the texture that are \"higher\" cover parts that are \"lower\" as the camera moves.\n4) It is a purely optical effect performed in the pixel shader without generating new polygons.\n\n**2. Explain the difference between simple Parallax Mapping and Steep Parallax Mapping.**\n**Answer:**\nSimple parallax mapping and steep parallax mapping differ in how they estimate UV offsets.\n1) Simple parallax mapping uses a single heightmap sample and shifts the UV coordinates along the view vector. It works for shallow depths but causes distortion at steep angles.\n2) Steep parallax mapping divides the heightmap into layers and steps along the view ray in tangent space, sampling the heightmap at each step.\n3) It stops stepping when the ray's depth exceeds the sampled height value, finding a much more accurate intersection point.\n4) Steep parallax mapping can render deep cracks and vertical walls but requires many texture samples.\n\n**3. What is Parallax Occlusion Mapping (POM) and how does it improve quality?**\n**Answer:**\nPOM is an extension of steep parallax mapping that interpolates between the last step before and the first step after the ray intersection.\n1) Steep parallax mapping creates visible stair-step artifacts (aliasing) in deep cracks because of discrete sampling steps.\n2) POM uses linear interpolation between the two height values to find a sub-step intersection point.\n3) This produces smooth, detailed depth curves without requiring hundreds of sampling steps.\n4) POM is the standard depth mapping technique for highly detailed textures like brick walls or cobblestone roads.\n\n**4. How does parallax mapping handle self-shadowing (silhouette shadows)?**\n**Answer:**\nSome parallax occlusion mapping implementations calculate self-shadowing by tracing a second ray from the intersection point to the light source.\n1) After finding the intersection point, the shader casts a ray in tangent space toward the light direction.\n2) It steps along this ray, comparing the ray's height to the heightmap values.\n3) If any point along the ray is higher than the starting point, the pixel is in shadow.\n4) This adds realistic shadows cast by stones and cracks onto neighboring low areas, but doubles the rendering cost.\n\n**5. What is displacement mapping and how does it differ from parallax mapping?**\n**Answer:**\nDisplacement mapping physically alters the geometry of a mesh, whereas parallax mapping only shifts texture coordinates.\n1) Displacement mapping reads a heightmap and displaces vertices along their normal vectors.\n2) It requires a highly tessellated mesh (millions of polygons) or GPU hardware tessellation to resolve fine details.\n3) Unlike parallax mapping, displacement mapping updates the mesh silhouette and works at extreme grazing angles.\n4) It is computationally expensive because it generates new geometry, whereas parallax mapping runs entirely inside the pixel shader.\n\n**6. What are the performance implications of Parallax Occlusion Mapping?**\n**Answer:**\nPOM is one of the most texture-bandwidth-heavy pixel shader techniques.\n1) Tracing rays requires a loop in the pixel shader that samples the heightmap texture multiple times (typically 8 to 32 steps).\n2) Dynamic branching is used to reduce steps for pixels viewed straight-on, but pixels viewed at steep angles hit the worst-case step count.\n3) It can significantly reduce frame rates on mobile or low-end GPUs due to texture cache misses and ALU load.\n4) Game developers optimize POM by reducing the step count based on distance from the camera.\n\n**7. Why does POM fail at the silhouette edges of a 3D model?**\n**Answer:**\nPOM fails at silhouette edges because it does not modify the actual geometry boundary of the 3D model.\n1) Since the mesh remains a flat polygon, the edge silhouette of the model remains completely straight and low-poly.\n2) If you look at a brick wall from a $90^\\circ$ angle, the edges will be perfectly flat even if the surface looks bumpy.\n3) In some advanced shaders, depth writing is used to discard pixels at silhouette boundaries, but this can cause sorting and performance issues.\n4) True silhouette deformation requires tessellation and displacement mapping.",
